\documentclass[10pt,a4paper]{article}
\usepackage[a4paper,margin=0.76in]{geometry}
\usepackage{fontspec}
\usepackage{xeCJK}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{array,tabularx}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage{xurl}
\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}
\setmainfont{DejaVu Sans}
\setCJKmainfont{Noto Sans CJK KR}
\setCJKmonofont{Noto Sans CJK KR}
\setlength{\parindent}{0pt}
\setlength{\parskip}{3.5pt}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.17}
\setlist[itemize]{leftmargin=1.5em,topsep=2pt,itemsep=2pt,parsep=0pt}
\newcolumntype{Y}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
\newcommand{\sectionline}[1]{\vspace{3pt}{\large\bfseries #1}\par\vspace{1pt}}
\begin{document}
\fontsize{10.2}{13.0}\selectfont
\begin{center}
{\Large\bfseries Week 38 -- Korean Language Modelling}\par\vspace{3pt}

\end{center}
\vspace{-5pt}

\sectionline{Quick summary}
I implemented an \textbf{interpolated trigram language model} for Korean questions and evaluated it alongside a unigram baseline and our existing add-$k$ bigram. My question was simple: \emph{will looking at two previous words help predict the next word better than looking at just one?}

\textbf{Main finding:} It did \textbf{not} help with the settings I tried. The bigram achieved \textbf{24.36 perplexity}, compared with \textbf{26.70} for the interpolated trigram. Another important observation is that \textbf{38.29\% of validation question tokens became \texttt{<UNK>}}. This makes some extremely low-perplexity examples misleading.

\sectionline{1. The idea behind the code}
The original bigram only considers the immediately preceding word. A trigram also considers the word before that, so in principle it can learn more specific question patterns. The downside is that exact three-word combinations appear less often, especially with a small dataset. I therefore did not use a plain trigram by itself: I combined unigram, bigram and trigram probabilities so that the model can still assign some probability when a particular trigram is rare or unseen.

\begin{center}
$P(w_i\mid w_{i-2},w_{i-1})
=0.15P_{\rm uni}(w_i)+0.35P_{\rm bi}(w_i\mid w_{i-1})
+0.50P_{\rm tri}(w_i\mid w_{i-2},w_{i-1}).$
\end{center}

The weights \textbf{(0.15, 0.35, 0.50)} were chosen as fixed starting values; I did \textbf{not} optimise them on validation examples. Each component uses add-$k$ smoothing with \textbf{$k=0.1$}, so an unseen combination does not receive zero probability.

In the script, I first prepare the shared Korean questions and map uncommon words to \texttt{<UNK>}. I then count individual tokens, bigrams and trigrams from training data. The probability functions use these counts, and the evaluator applies all three models to the same validation sequences. The code also runs sanity checks and saves scores for each question in a CSV. No neural network or pretrained language model is involved here.

\sectionline{2. Data and evaluation setup}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Data:} 2,422 Korean training questions and 356 Korean validation questions, loaded from the group's shared dataset. We do not use context passages for this task.
\item \textbf{Tokenisation:} Split each question on whitespace, matching the existing bigram experiment. Keep a token only if it appears at least twice in training; all other words become \texttt{<UNK>}.
\item \textbf{Vocabulary:} 1,126 prediction tokens, including \texttt{<UNK>} and \texttt{<EOS>}. A beginning marker (\texttt{<BOS>}) is used only as history, not as a prediction target; the trigram gets two beginning markers.
\item \textbf{Metric:} Corpus-level per-token perplexity, calculated from all target log probabilities combined. We score each question's words and one \texttt{<EOS>}, but not the beginning markers: \textbf{1,729 text tokens + 356 EOS = 2,085 scored tokens}. Lower perplexity is better.
\end{itemize}

\sectionline{3. What the numbers show}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{p{9.5cm}r}
\hline
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Validation perplexity}\\
\hline
Unigram (no context) & 46.5771\\
Add-$k$ bigram (one previous token) & \textbf{24.3639}\\
Interpolated trigram (up to two previous tokens) & 26.6954\\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The trigram's perplexity was about \textbf{9.6\% higher} than the bigram's. The result goes against my initial hypothesis, but it gives us a useful comparison: extra context is not automatically better. Sparse three-token counts are a possible explanation; we have \emph{not yet measured} how many validation trigrams were unseen, so I would not present sparsity as a confirmed cause.

\newpage
\sectionline{4. Looking at real questions}
The first five lowest-perplexity questions in the \emph{unfiltered} output were surprisingly all made up of unknown tokens after vocabulary mapping. For example, question \textbf{\#203}, ``How many years did the Etruscan dynasty rule Rome?'', had \textbf{6/6 UNK tokens} and a perplexity of just \textbf{3.95}. Questions \#53, \#198, \#115 and \#154 were also completely UNK after mapping. A low score here does \emph{not} mean the model knows those original Korean words: it is scoring their shared \texttt{<UNK>} replacements.

I therefore made a \textbf{separate diagnostic list} of distinct questions with at most \textbf{25\% UNK}. This filter was used only to interpret examples, \textbf{not to change the reported validation perplexity}.

\vspace{1pt}\textbf{Five lowest-perplexity questions after that filter:}
\begin{center}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}rY r@{}}
\hline
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Question (Korean; English meaning)} & \textbf{PPL}\\
\hline
66 & 세상에서 가장 큰 군대는 무엇인가?\\[-1pt]
 & \emph{What is the largest army in the world?} & 4.82\\
225 & 창원시에서 가장 높은 건물은 무엇인가?\\[-1pt]
 & \emph{What is the tallest building in Changwon?} & 7.68\\
144 & 미얀마에서 가장 큰 도시는 무엇인가?\\[-1pt]
 & \emph{What is the largest city in Myanmar?} & 7.76\\
286 & 한국에서 가장 큰 공방은 무엇인가?\\[-1pt]
 & \emph{What is the largest workshop in Korea?} & 8.16\\
205 & 세상에서 가장 오래된 시집은 무엇인가?\\[-1pt]
 & \emph{What is the oldest poetry collection in the world?} & 8.68\\
\hline
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

These mostly use familiar comparative/superlative question structures (``largest'', ``highest'', ``oldest''). That is \emph{consistent with} learning repeated question templates. There was an identical copy of question \#66 at \#299; I excluded it from this display so the five examples are distinct, but \textbf{both rows remain in the actual evaluation}.

\vspace{1pt}\textbf{Five highest-perplexity questions (same original evaluation):}
\begin{center}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}rY c r@{}}
\hline
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{English meaning} & \textbf{UNK} & \textbf{PPL}\\
\hline
73 & When was the Bible written? & 0/3 & 259.66\\
347 & Which present-day country was the Ming dynasty in? & 1/4 & 212.39\\
191 & Which US state is smallest by area? & 1/6 & 206.92\\
125 & What is South Africa's founding date? & 1/4 & 191.56\\
163 & How many Crusades took place in total? & 1/5 & 184.38\\
\hline
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\sectionline{5. My interpretation and what matters for the group}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The main hypothesis was not supported.} With these fixed interpolation weights and this training split, the bigram predicted validation tokens more confidently overall than my interpolated trigram.
\item \textbf{UNK is a major caveat.} Exactly 662 of 1,729 validation text tokens (38.29\%) mapped to \texttt{<UNK>}. Easy-to-predict UNK patterns can make a question look much easier than its original words would be.
\item \textbf{UNK alone does not explain high perplexity.} The Bible question (\#73) had \emph{zero} unknown tokens but the highest perplexity, 259.66. Rare word \emph{sequences} or conditional probabilities could matter too; we would need token-count inspection to confirm why.
\item \textbf{The comparison is fair within my script.} All three models use the same 356 questions, vocabulary and scoring window. The reported figures are corpus perplexities, \emph{not} means of the individual-question scores.
\end{itemize}


\end{document}
